+# Performance report: BlockInfo (block counts panel and hover highlight)
+
+**Workload**: a client MicroProfiler capture on desktop with about 6,000 placed blocks, plus FPS on
+a phone with the same build. Both numbers come from you. The "after" numbers below have not been
+measured yet because this change was made without access to Studio.
+
+**Bottleneck**: Luau script time on the client, plus garbage collection. In the old
+`BlockInfo.client.luau`, the `RenderStepped` handler did work every frame that grew with the
+number of blocks:
+
+1. `blocksFolder:GetChildren()` ran twice per frame: once to count blocks by type and once for
+   the total. Each call builds a new table with every block in it, so at 6,000 blocks that is
+   roughly 12,000 array slots allocated per frame. That garbage is the source of the GC label
+   that showed up after `Script_BlockInfo`.
+2. `findHoveredBlock` called `GetChildren()` a third time and then ran `IsA` and a distance check
+   on every block to find the one nearest the mouse.
+3. A new `RaycastParams` and a new panel string, built by repeated `..` concatenation, were
+   created every frame, and `countsLabel.Text` was set every frame even when nothing had changed.
+
+That comes to about three full passes over the folder per frame, including about 6,000 engine
+calls, which matches the roughly 2.5 ms you saw. The cost grows in a straight line with build
+size, so a 20,000 block build would cost about 8 ms per frame from this script alone, before
+counting GC.
+
+| Metric | Before | After |
+|---|---|---|
+| `Script_BlockInfo` per frame, desktop, about 6,000 blocks | about 2.5 ms | not measured yet. Expected well under 0.1 ms and flat as builds grow |
+| GC label after `Script_BlockInfo` | most frames | not measured yet. Expected rare |
+| Phone FPS, same build | about 35 | not measured yet |
+
+**Changes** (biggest lever first):
+
+- **Counts are updated from events instead of recounted each frame.** Per-type counts and the
+  total are adjusted in `ChildAdded` and `ChildRemoved` on `Workspace.Blocks`, with one
+  `GetChildren()` pass at startup. When nothing is placed or removed, counting costs nothing.
+- **Hover uses a spatial query instead of a full scan.** After the existing raycast,
+  `Workspace:GetPartBoundsInRadius(hitPoint, HOVER_RADIUS)`, filtered to the Blocks folder,
+  returns only the few blocks near the hit point. The same nearest-center rule is applied to
+  those. Every block whose center is within `HOVER_RADIUS` of the hit point has bounds inside
+  that sphere, so the set of blocks that can be picked is the same as before.
+- **The panel text is rebuilt only when something changed**: a count changed or the hovered
+  block changed. The selection box `Adornee` is also written only when the hovered block changes.
+- **`RaycastParams` and `OverlapParams` are created once** and reused. The character filter is
+  updated only when the character changes.
+- The handler is wrapped in `debug.profilebegin("BlockInfo:Step")` so it shows up under its own
+  name in the MicroProfiler and Script Profiler.
+
+**Behavior**: unchanged. The panel shows the same lines in the same order and format, and the
+hover picks the block with the nearest center within `HOVER_RADIUS` of the point under the
+mouse, as before. Two edge cases that should not show up in practice:
+
+- If two blocks are exactly the same distance from the hit point, the old code picked whichever
+  came first in the folder, and the new code picks whichever the spatial query returns first.
+  Both are at the same distance, so either one is a reasonable pick.
+- Counts use the block's name when it is added and when it is removed. A block renamed while it
+  sits in the folder would be counted under its old name. Nothing renames blocks today
+  (`BlockPlacement.server.luau` sets the name before parenting). Spatial queries also skip
+  parts with `CanQuery` off, and blocks are always created with the default (on).
+
+**Tried and reverted**: nothing. No before and after measurement was possible in this session.
+
+**Left alone**: the raycast still runs every frame. A single raycast costs microseconds and
+doesn't grow with build size, and limiting it to frames where the mouse or camera moved would
+make the highlight lag behind moving blocks or characters. The server-side placement code was
+not part of the problem.
+
+**For you to verify in Studio**:
+
+1. Load or place the same build of about 6,000 blocks, start a playtest, and take a client
+   MicroProfiler capture from the same camera position as before. Find `BlockInfo:Step`
+   (it replaces most of `Script_BlockInfo`) and check that it is a small fraction of a
+   millisecond and that the GC label after it is gone or much rarer.
+2. Double the build (about 12,000 blocks) and check that `BlockInfo:Step` stays about the same.
+   If it does, the cost no longer depends on build size.
+3. Check the behavior: counts go up when you place each block type, `Total` matches, the
+   yellow box and `Pointing at:` follow the mouse onto blocks and clear when you point at the
+   baseplate or the sky. The highlight should also show up when you point at the ground right
+   next to a block, as it did before.
+4. On the phone, publish and play the same build and compare FPS with the 35 you saw. Studio
+   runs on a desktop, so it can't show phone frame rate. If the phone is still below target
+   after this, take a MicroProfiler capture on the phone. The next cost at 6,000 or more blocks
+   is more likely rendering (draw calls, especially the transparent Glass windows) than this
+   script.
